From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Clint Goudie-Nice <clint-goudie-nice@example.net>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:32:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] l2mcorch: count a port's group members by what the chip
 holds, and drop them with the port

Port::m_l2mc_count decides whether PortsOrch::removeBridgePort() may remove a
bridge port, but L2mcOrch kept it only on the group-member path, while the
SAI members it creates for multicast router ports went uncounted. A port that
is a group member and a router port at once (a switch's uplink with the
reserved groups statically flooded to every port; any port on which l2mcd
hears a query) loses track when it goes down:

- RemoveL2mcGroupMember() keeps the SAI member for the router port and returns
  without decrementing the count or the group's ref_count;
- RemoveL2mcMrouterPort() then removes that SAI member, decrementing neither;
- a member l2mcd reports in a group the port was in only as a router port is
  "already there": no ref_count, no count, and its later delete decrements a
  count that was never raised.

Seen on a Celestica DX010 (Tomahawk-1) on 202511 with this series: a port
whose link dropped while it was both was left with m_l2mc_count 4 and no L2MC
group member on its bridge port in ASIC_DB, and every entry it had been in
with a ref_count that never reaches zero.

The next time the port left its last VLAN, removeBridgePort() set the bridge
port admin down, removed its STP ports, and only then refused on the count;
doVlanMemberTask() kept the delete and retried it twice a second, and the
VLAN added next (a native VLAN move is a delete then an add) reused the admin
down bridge port: no learning, the port dead until a swss restart. The
retried delete also took the port out of a VLAN of the same name added back
behind it. The refusal strands a port without the leak too, whenever l2mcd's
member deletes reach orchagent after the VLAN member delete.

- m_l2mc_count is now every SAI L2MC group member on the port's bridge port,
  whichever path created it (countMember(), in PortsOrch's own copy); a group's
  ref_count counts the members with is_l2mc_member, so a router port's member
  becoming a group member, or ceasing to be one while the router port stays,
  moves the ref_count and not the SAI member.
- removeL2mcPortMembers() drops a port's members in one VLAN, or in all of
  them, with the port's place in the router-port lists, without waiting for
  l2mcd: removeVlanMember() calls it for the VLAN the port leaves, and
  removeBridgePort() for every VLAN before it changes anything of the bridge
  port. A group left without a member goes, as on a leave; l2mcd's late
  deletes find nothing. A count left above zero with no member is logged and
  reset rather than refused.
- PortsOrch's copies of the port and the VLAN take L2mcOrch's counts before
  they are written back.
- doL2mcMrouterTask() lists a router port once: a retried SET pushed it again,
  and the copy outlived the port's removal from the list.

Tests: L2mcOrchTest (the DX010's sequence, a router port leaving before the
member, a port leaving its VLAN with members left, a port leaving one of two
VLANs). Before this change all four fail, the first two with the counts above
and the third with the bridge port reused admin down.
